      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(infra): return undefined for bigint exceeding MAX_SAFE_INTEGER in normalizeSqliteNumber",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\n`normalizeSqliteNumber` converts SQLite `bigint` columns to JavaScript `number` via `Number(value)`. For bigint values exceeding `Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER` (2^53-1), `Number()` silently loses low-bit precision (e.g. `Number(9007199254740993n)` → `9007199254740992`). For extremely large values, it returns `Infinity`, which can cause downstream threshold comparisons to produce incorrect results (e.g. expiration timestamps, byte counts).\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nReturn `undefined` when a bigint value exceeds the safe integer range, instead of silently truncating or producing `Infinity`. This lets callers handle the out-of-range case explicitly rather than acting on a corrupted numeric value.\n\n## User Impact\n\nIf a SQLite bigint column ever contains a value beyond 2^53-1, the function now returns `undefined` instead of a silently-wrong number. In practice, SQLite rowids and counts rarely exceed this range, so the change is defensive rather than fixing a current user-facing bug.\n\n## Evidence\n\n```text\n$ npx vitest run src/infra/sqlite-number.test.ts\n Test Files  1 passed (1)\n      Tests  11 passed (11)\n```\n\nNew test cases:\n- `returns undefined for bigint exceeding MAX_SAFE_INTEGER`\n- `returns undefined for bigint below -MAX_SAFE_INTEGER`\n\n## AI-assisted\n\nNo.",
